# Chapter 11 — Why the World Builds Artificial Intelligence

*An answer needs an occasion, a process, and a world in which to matter.*

Imagine a trained language model loaded into a computer. The equipment is powered, its parameters are available, and the software is ready to receive a request. For this illustration, there is no scheduled task, sensor feed, or continuing agent process. Nobody submits anything. The model does not begin reconsidering yesterday's answer. It does not become bored, invent an occupation, or decide that its electricity bill needs paying. The service may perform housekeeping, but the stored capacity is not conducting an inquiry.

Then a request arrives. Software prepares the input, computations run, and an answer begins to appear. The same learned organization that accomplished nothing while waiting can now distinguish, compose, compare, or help solve a problem. Its capability was real before the request. Its exercise required an event and an executing system.

The distinction matters to the creation-cycle account of AI. Existing production supplies resources for learning; constraints and additional support make particular capabilities accessible; training assembles learned organization; useful deployment may help renew the wider system that builds and operates it. That system can then support further training and construction. The model's presence is one stage of the cycle. Its maintenance and reproduction depend on operations extending well beyond the stored parameters.

Much of the present chapter concerns language models used to generate responses. Artificial intelligence also includes other learning systems and controllers, with different operating arrangements. We should not turn a feature of a chat interface into a universal definition of intelligence. Nevertheless, the waiting model makes several boundaries unusually clear: the learned model, the computation using it, the program organizing repeated computations, and the productive organization that supplies the entire operation.

“Without a prompt it does nothing” captures something important about a model that has not been invoked. It becomes misleading if it means that every action by every AI system requires a new message from a human. A clock, a camera, another program, or an ongoing controller can provide the next input. A model's previous output can help determine what the enclosing program does next. Researchers have demonstrated systems that interleave generated reasoning with actions and observations from external environments.[1]

Once such a loop is running, a person need not intervene at every step. The loop still has a physical implementation, a policy for continuation, access to particular observations and actions, and conditions under which it stops. Persistence has been organized. A model generating an instruction to continue does not by itself procure the hardware and energy needed to execute that instruction. The boundary has moved from a single response to a larger system, and the larger system must be explained.

The same care applies to purpose. A deployed task can be supplied by an operator, inherited from a training objective, or generated within a continuing program. That is enough for a machine to perform an extended undertaking without settling whether it has desires, subjective experience, or a biological interest in staying alive. A capacity to pursue an objective and an organization that regenerates itself are different achievements.

To understand how the capacity arose, return to the book's two-sided description of systems. The model is materially realized: equipment, memory, electrical processes, data storage, and the work of building and operating them. It is also formally organized: architecture, representations, numerical parameters, objectives, and procedures determine relations among inputs and outputs. The Platonic aspect names those relations. It does not place a working intelligence in a realm where physical execution is unnecessary.

An equation describing a network, a file containing its parameters, and a running service are therefore different stages of an undertaking. The equation specifies an organization. Training changes values within an organization according to a procedure and encountered data. A retained parameter file preserves part of the result. Inference uses that result to perform further computation. Deployment connects the computation to people, equipment, or other programs capable of doing something with its output.

Each transition can fail. A promising architecture can be unaffordable to train. Training performance can fail to generalize. A useful model can be impossible to serve within the required latency or cost. A correct answer can arrive where nobody can act on it. These failures belong to the theory because they identify which constraints keep a formal possibility from becoming an effective material capability.

Training supplies a particularly clear case of constrained search. Investigators do not sample uniformly from every possible intelligent program. Architecture determines which organizations can be represented. Data makes some distinctions available and leaves others absent. Objectives and update procedures influence which changes are retained. Evaluations determine which accomplishments are recognized as improvements. These constraints can make a useful result accessible while also making certain errors persistent.

This is the problem of chapter 1 on a larger scale. A bottleneck can concentrate work on a tractable distinction. It can also remove the information or freedom required for success. A narrowly constructed learning task may produce an excellent score by rewarding a shortcut that fails outside the task. The existence of constraints explains neither success nor failure until their relation to the desired capability is specified.

The process also requires support before it yields a return. Chapter 5 distinguished making an engine cheaper to operate from committing enough available energy to make a costly first engine usable. Artificial learning permits the same distinction. A better method may reduce the resources needed to acquire a capability. A larger commitment of existing resources may make an expensive learning process possible. The routes can interact, but the accounting must retain the difference.

The physical implementation of learning makes the joint material and Platonic claim unusually direct. A formal operation specifies a relationship among possible inputs and outputs. An actual device realizes it through physical states and transitions. Stochastic thermodynamics of computation studies the costs of those realizations, including their dependence on how a device is operated and the inputs it encounters.[2] A logical description alone therefore leaves part of the explanation open.

This connection also clarifies what an improvement can mean. A useful learned representation may let a later task be completed with fewer attempts, even though acquiring that representation was expensive. Faster or more economical inference does not undo the physical work of training. To evaluate the chapter's proposed development, count the supported learning interval, subsequent use, and replacement of its supports over a stated horizon. Success is a changed capability with a traceable cost and consequence, rather than an increase in computation taken as its own justification.

Training does not pay its own bill merely because it may eventually be useful. Equipment, power, human work, prepared data, and unsuccessful attempts must be supported through an interval in which the hoped-for return is absent. Society supplies that support through existing productive arrangements. The trained capability may subsequently alter those arrangements, but the later contribution cannot be assumed at the moment expenditure begins.

Inference then presents a different resource problem. Once ordinary inference begins with a frozen model, its learned parameters remain fixed during that computation. The model does not automatically rewrite its weights whenever it produces a longer answer. But fixed parameters do not mean that the total computation spent on an answer is fixed.

In autoregressive generation, an output is produced through successive steps, with earlier generated symbols available as input to later ones. The original Transformer paper describes this kind of generation within its sequence architecture.[3] A longer run performs additional computation. A system can also generate several candidates, evaluate intermediate steps, use tools, or revise a proposed solution. Those operations have costs beyond a single short response. Different models and implementations organize those costs differently.

The distinction is between the machinery and the amount of work performed with it. A fixed calculator can execute one arithmetic operation or a long sequence of them. That does not make every language model a reliable calculator. It shows why a fixed set of parameters alone cannot establish a single, unchanging limit on the difficulty of every task it can complete. Organization of the computation matters alongside the model that performs individual steps.

Every actual attempt nevertheless has a finite allocation: hardware, time, memory, permitted context, and a policy governing how long execution continues. Increasing that allocation changes the attempt. It does not make resources infinite. Nor is a token a standard unit of thought or a constant quantity of electrical work. Counting generated tokens can describe one aspect of an experiment without measuring cognition itself.

This brings us to the label “thinking mode.” The phrase can conceal several mechanisms. A careful account must ask what changes when the mode is enabled. Is the prompt different? Was the model trained to produce and use intermediate reasoning? Does the system permit more generation, search across candidates, or call external tools? The interface label alone cannot answer those questions.

Prompting is a real part of the story. Wei and colleagues showed that examples containing intermediate reasoning could improve performance on tested reasoning tasks. Kojima and colleagues found that the instruction “Let's think step by step” could elicit improved performance without worked examples in their tested setting.[4] Here an instruction changes how an existing model is used. No new parameter training is required for that particular intervention.

Calling this prompt hacking can express a reasonable objection to treating an elicitation trick as the appearance of an entirely new faculty. The important question is what the trick actually accomplishes. If intermediate steps let a fixed model solve a problem it previously failed, the operational improvement is real. Describing the intervention as a prompt does not erase the computation it elicits. Describing the output as thought does not establish that the computation is reliable.

The word “just” becomes inaccurate when it collapses every reasoning system into that one mechanism. DeepSeek-R1 research reports reinforcement learning intended to develop reasoning behavior on verifiable tasks.[5] Training of this kind changes the learned policy before the particular inference run. It is a different intervention from appending an instruction to an unchanged model, even when the eventual system also uses prompts and a larger inference budget.

There are also methods remarkably close to the prompt-hacking description. The s1 study combined supervised training on reasoning examples with a generation intervention that could extend a response by appending “Wait” when the model attempted to finish. The authors reported improvements on their evaluated tasks.[6] The striking cue should not conceal either part of the method: there was training, and there was a procedure governing the amount of subsequent computation. A word did not create an unlimited new source of intelligence.

Additional inference computation can improve results, and its allocation can matter substantially. Snell and colleagues investigated ways of spending test-time computation through search and response revision, finding that useful allocation depended on problem difficulty.[7] This supports a bounded claim: the same starting capability can perform differently when its execution is organized differently. It supplies no general promise that doubling a budget doubles understanding, or that an arbitrarily long run will solve any problem.

The limits become clearer when the task changes. Imagine asking a model to identify a measurement that has never been supplied to it and cannot be inferred from the available information. A longer response cannot turn an absent observation into evidence. It can propose possibilities or explain what measurement is needed. To distinguish those possibilities, the system must obtain information through some additional operation. Generating another paragraph is not a substitute for that operation.

In another hypothetical case, the information is present but the model repeatedly applies a mistaken procedure. More computation may provide time to discover the error. It may also elaborate the same mistake. If several candidates inherit the same blind spot, agreement among them will not make the conclusion true. If the method used to rank candidates shares the error, search can reinforce a bad answer. The useful quantity is successful problem solving under specified conditions, not the length or confidence of the explanation.

Self-correction is therefore a capability to test. Huang and colleagues found that the models and reasoning tasks they studied often failed to improve through intrinsic self-correction without external feedback, and sometimes became worse.[8] Their result does not prove that every later model must fail at correcting itself. It demonstrates why the instruction to reconsider cannot serve as evidence that reconsideration succeeded. The date, task, model, and available feedback belong in the claim.

A reliable external check can change the situation. Arithmetic can be checked by an appropriate calculation; a program can be executed against relevant tests; a candidate formal proof can be checked within its formal system. Such checks have their own scope. Passing a test suite does not establish every property of a program, and a proof of a statement in a specified system does not establish that the statement describes a factory correctly. Nevertheless, a check can supply a constraint that the model's own fluent judgment lacks. The learned-search and formal-verification combination introduced in chapter 10 illustrates the distinction.[9]

The relationship is cybernetic when observations of consequences alter subsequent action. A machine proposes, acts or tests, observes, and changes its next proposal. Merely generating a sentence saying that the proposal has been checked does not close that causal circuit. Some procedures can perform useful internal checks; others need an observation or an independently reliable operation. The question is what information returns, what it corrects, and what errors can still pass through.

Learning also needs to be located correctly. During ordinary frozen-model inference, changing the context changes what the model can use without changing its parameters. A program can preserve a result in external memory and supply it on a later occasion. A separate training process can update the weights. These are different forms of retention with different costs and consequences. An answer becoming more informed over a conversation does not by itself demonstrate permanent retraining, and an agent storing a note does not thereby redesign its neural architecture.

There is consequently no inference from a “thinking” setting to infinite cognitive growth. Some tasks may benefit from much longer search; some reach a plateau; some deteriorate under an ineffective continuation policy. The claim that more time can help is compatible with all three outcomes. The stronger claim that more time must eventually remove every limitation needs a mechanism that prompting, a fixed model, and an arbitrary budget increase do not supply.

The model's dependence on a wider organization is equally concrete. A saved checkpoint preserves learned structure. It does not maintain the chip that reads it, cool the building, repair a transformer, manufacture replacement equipment, or ensure that the people performing those tasks are fed. An agent can be connected to purchasing, monitoring, and maintenance procedures. If those connections contribute to renewal, they matter. Their existence must be shown rather than inferred from the agent's ability to discuss maintenance.

Chapter 10's intertwined account now gives us a sharper boundary. A deployed AI can participate in cybernetic regulation without the model itself constituting an autopoietic organization. Under the book's definition, the continuing assembly is the wider system that repeatedly produces and supports the capability while using it to enable energy access. Identify its regenerative network and how the model participates in that return. Frequent invocations or many copies of a checkpoint alone do not establish it. The same requirement applies whether initiation comes from a person, a timer, or another program.

Dependence alone does not distinguish machines from everything living. Organisms also require outside matter and energy. The relevant distinction concerns the organization of renewal: what produces the components and relationships through which the system continues? A cell participates in producing its own boundary and metabolic organization. A model invoked by a service does not automatically undertake an equivalent process. Neither the presence of an input nor the absence of a human prompt settles the question.

The book's thesis can accommodate these limits because its proposed direction concerns the assembly of effective pathways. A bounded machine can change the search available to a larger system. It can narrow a set of candidates, recognize a pattern, or propose a construction that people could not practically examine unaided. The contribution need not include a complete self-model or an independent intention to contribute. Its value depends on what the surrounding organization can do with it.

The proposed energetic preference explains why this cycle might favor useful artificial cognition. A learned capability that improves access to usable energy or the reliability of production can help support the organization maintaining and reproducing it. The higher assembly comprises the model, its execution, and the productive relations through which its results become effective. A later model's larger electricity bill is not the predicted gain; the gain is greater sustained extraction or access by the specified wider arrangement after its added costs are counted.[10]

A learning-loss gradient must remain distinct from a physical gradient. A lower error score does not measure additional useful work, and burning more electricity on a useless answer does not demonstrate the predicted constructive development. The account needs to trace particular improvements into particular physical consequences, including their costs and failures. Money, benchmark performance, energy throughput, and entropy-production rates cannot substitute for one another in that calculation.

The emergence hypothesis is therefore a claim about successive cycles: supported learning assembles a capacity, deployment creates a return, regenerative operations retain and reproduce it, and the new capacity changes what can be attempted next. An energy boost can come from expanded infrastructure; a new constraint can come from the computational demand the previous generation created. This is the proposed natural emergence of AI through endogenous changes as well as external events. Each connection can fail, including the return from useful answers to physical production.

A useful test would compare the same model on defined tasks under different prompts and inference budgets, then separately vary access to external feedback. Record correctness, the work performed, elapsed time, and relevant resource costs. Include failures and plateaus. Compare those changes with gains from different training. This would reveal whether an improvement came from eliciting an existing capability, acquiring a new learned organization, supplying additional evidence, or spending more computation. It would prevent the single word “thinking” from doing the explanatory work.

Existing logs can sometimes support these comparisons without a new trial. A policy cutoff or externally imposed change in resource allocation might provide useful variation if the assignment process is defensible. Simply observing that hard requests receive more compute would confound resource effects with task difficulty. Model versions, task selection, tool access, and shared infrastructure must be accounted for. Historical data are therefore a possible source of causal evidence, not an automatic verdict on whether more thinking helps.

The broader theory needs further comparisons. Does a useful cognitive gain actually change production or resource access? Can a different method produce the same result more cheaply? Do histories with the proposed prerequisites repeatedly generate relevant capabilities through substitute routes, or can those conditions persist without the predicted development? A model of profitability or inherited technical practice may explain a case without the proposed thermodynamic principle. The principle earns its place by explaining or predicting something those accounts leave unresolved.

The waiting machine has now answered. The output may be correct, useful, misleading, or immaterial. Its generation has used finite resources through an organized procedure. Whether it initiates another step depends on the program and circumstances in which it operates. Whether its result changes the world depends on a longer chain of evaluation and action.

Those limits define the next problem. A capability must be connected to an organization that can supply it, maintain it, and put it to work. The next chapter follows that connection into data centers and physical production. The question is how much the world can change through bounded cognitive machinery, and whether that change can help renew the material conditions from which further cognition is assembled.

