1. Shunyu Yao and colleagues, [“ReAct: Synergizing Reasoning and Acting in Language Models”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03629), 2022; ICLR 2023. [S100](sources.md#s100). The opening idle service is an illustration with its execution conditions specified, not a claim about all AI systems.

2. David H. Wolpert, [“Stochastic thermodynamics of computation”](https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.05669) (2019), connects logical maps to physical implementations and thermodynamic costs. No universal joules-per-thought measure or entropy-maximizing learning law follows. See [S122](sources.md#s122).

3. Ashish Vaswani and colleagues, [“Attention Is All You Need”](https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762), 2017, section 3. [S102](sources.md#s102). Autoregressive generation illustrates repeated computation; this chapter makes no claim that every AI architecture has identical inference costs.

4. Jason Wei and colleagues, [“Chain-of-Thought Prompting Elicits Reasoning in Large Language Models”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.11903), 2022; Takeshi Kojima and colleagues, [“Large Language Models Are Zero-Shot Reasoners”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.11916), 2022. [S95](sources.md#s95), [S96](sources.md#s96). The elicitation results are task- and model-dependent.

5. DeepSeek-AI and colleagues, [“DeepSeek-R1: Incentivizing Reasoning Capability in LLMs via Reinforcement Learning”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12948), first released 2025. [S97](sources.md#s97). The reported training mechanism refutes an account of all reasoning behavior as prompt modification alone.

6. Niklas Muennighoff and colleagues, [“s1: Simple Test-Time Scaling”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.19393), 2025. [S99](sources.md#s99). The study combined training and budget forcing; the continuation cue alone is not the whole method.

7. Charlie Snell and colleagues, [“Scaling LLM Test-Time Compute Optimally Can Be More Effective than Scaling Model Parameters”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.03314), 2024. [S98](sources.md#s98). The reported allocation effects do not establish unrestricted scaling of cognition.

8. Jie Huang and colleagues, [“Large Language Models Cannot Self-Correct Reasoning Yet”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.01798), 2023; ICLR 2024. [S101](sources.md#s101). The manuscript preserves the study's limited scope; it does not generalize its result to all future models.

9. Thomas Hubert and colleagues, [“Olympiad-Level Formal Mathematical Reasoning with Reinforcement Learning”](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-09833-y), 2025. [S93](sources.md#s93). The other verification examples and proposed comparisons are explanatory illustrations and research designs.

10. The energetic distinctions follow chapter 2 and [S07](sources.md#s07), [S68](sources.md#s68). The interpretation of AI as natural emergence and the comparisons proposed here are the book's hypotheses and research agenda, not findings established by the language-model papers.

